--TEST--
Every escape that writes the `ESC` byte (`\e`, `\x1b`, `\033`, `\u{1b}`) does not compile, in a double-quoted string, an interpolated one, a heredoc and an html template, and the error is at the escape itself — rule:tooling/an-escape-in-source-text-is-a-compile-error
--FILE--
<?nvs
string $name = "Ada";
// Each of the four escapes writes the `ESC` byte, so each is an error.
echo "\e[31mred", "\n";
echo "\x1b[1mbold", "\n";
echo "\033[4munder", "\n";
echo "\u{1b}[0m", "\n";
// The same escapes inside an interpolated string, a heredoc and an html template.
echo "hello \e[1m$name", "\n";
echo <<<TEXT
    hello \x1b[1m{$name}
    TEXT;
echo html`<b>\033[1m{$name}</b>`;
// A string without the `ESC` byte compiles.
echo "\x1c\\e[31m", "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
